2.bug_scenario:
In the VB.NET grammar, any string literal that contains an “@” symbol in its text isn’t being recognized as a string. On the test page you can see that:

  bob = new SqlCommand("Select * from test Where Code=@Code");

breaks the highlighting (the opening quote is not picked up), while a similar string without “@” works. Under the hood VB.NET’s grammar either inherits or re-defines a string pattern that doesn’t allow the “@” character, so the regex for matching double-quoted strings fails as soon as it sees “@.”
